# Shell scripts are checked out on Windows machines with core.autocrlf=true
# and get silently rewritten to CRLF, which then get COPY'd verbatim into
# Linux Docker images as docker-entrypoint.sh. The container's shell chokes
# on the trailing \r (e.g. `set -e` becomes `set -e\r`, producing
# "illegal option --" or "bad interpreter" and a crash-loop) — found the hard
# way across every plane's entrypoint scripts. Force LF regardless of local
# autocrlf settings so this cannot recur on a fresh Windows checkout.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Makefiles have the same problem with a sharper edge: a recipe line ending in
# a backslash continuation needs the backslash IMMEDIATELY before the newline.
# With CRLF the CR sits in between, so the continuation stops joining lines and
# the recipe runs as fragments (or dies on 'missing separator'). These are all
# stored LF in the blob already; this pins the CHECKOUT so a Windows clone with
# autocrlf=true cannot rewrite them.
Makefile text eol=lf
makefile text eol=lf
GNUmakefile text eol=lf
*.mk text eol=lf
